This review evaluates the CAPSICUM Phase-1 design-spar from the perspective of a standards and ontology skeptic.1. PositionEndorse-with-amendments. The ontological foundation is robust, but the transition from a formal "Logical Model of Endeavour" to a production RDF/SHACL/JSON-LD contract contains structural "wallpaper cracks" that risk breaking v1.0 within twelve months if not addressed.  2. Single Biggest Hidden RiskThe Namespace Fragility Trap. The spike uses a flat, unversioned namespace (https://capsicum.plausibleba.org/ns/core#). In the context of the Governed State Machine (GSM), where transitions depend on exact semantic subsumption (ε₁), any change to the ontology's structure without a URI-level versioning strategy or a stable dereferencing policy will break existing agent-governance kernels. This isn't just a technical debt; it's a governance failure that allows for "semantic drift" where an agent believes it is authorised based on a stale definition.  3. SHACL Coverage Assessment (A)SHACL is a powerful validation tool, but its coverage of CAPSICUM's Terms (Information × Governance) is uneven:Clean Coverage: Cardinality, range/domain, and basic "well-formedness" of Capabilities and Stages are handled natively by SHACL core.  Partial Coverage: The AgentCharter authority boundary can be validated for structural integrity, but the "constitutive" nature of the Governance column—where action is blocked unless explicitly permitted—requires SHACL-SPARQL to verify the absence of conflicting deontic norms (ε₂).  No Coverage: SHACL cannot natively model the temporal dynamics of the GSM's state transitions (δ). While it can validate a snapshot of the claims-settlement state, it cannot enforce the "Governed" part of the state machine without being wrapped in a custom runtime that manages state delta evaluation.  4. @context Durability Assessment (B)The spike's @context is "v0-ready" but fragile for a published open-core contract.  Concerns: The use of @vocab is avoided (good), but the container definitions (e.g., realisedBy as @set) are hardcoded, which might conflict with future multi-graph or multi-user needs in Layer 3.  Recommendation: Publish a v0 shape that explicitly separates the domain terminology (e.g., sp:Capability) from the governance machinery (e.g., cap:AgentCharter) using scoped contexts to prevent name collisions as the ontology expands to the full 9-cell matrix.  5. Metamodel Fidelity (C)Is orthogonality preserved? Fidelity preserved. The spike maintains the independence of the Domain and Governance columns, ensuring a change in licence status doesn't force a change in the domain entity.  Is vertical alignment derivable? Fidelity at risk. The current RDF shape lacks explicit "derivation rules" to link the Purpose layer (Set/Plan/Realise) to the Execution layer (Domain/Behaviour/Governance).  Is the GSM nine-tuple addressable? Fidelity preserved. The core elements (S, Σ, E) are clearly mapped in the capsicum.ttl file.  Are deontic operators explicit? Fidelity broken. The current spike collapses permissions and prohibitions into a flat "classification" string (e.g., "HumanPrimary"), losing the formal force of the tri-valued validity function V {Fire, Reject, Escalate}.  6. BACM Round-trip (D)Plausible. The externalised-Outcome pattern (BACM CAP-7) is successfully mirrored in the cap:Outcome and cap:Stage relationship. However, the round-trip is partially-blocked by the lack of a standardized way to represent the "Agentic Enablement Score (AES)" in BACM v1.0, which may require a custom extension profile.  7. Vertical-alignment Derivation Rules (E)There is a credible path, but it requires moving beyond static SHACL.Sketch: Use SPARQL Construct rules to derive operational Capabilities from strategic Means defined in the Purpose layer.  Strategic Implication: If this isn't credible, the "agentic-enterprise" positioning fails because the agent's authorised action space will always be a manual, high-maintenance "overlay" rather than a constitutive part of the enterprise architecture.  8. One Concrete Thing to Spike Before LockingSpike a "Conflict Trigger" (ε₂): Create a fixture with two overlapping AgentCharters for the same Capability—one granting permission and one imposing a prohibition based on an aesScore threshold. If the SHACL/SPARQL validator can't deterministically flag this as a "Deontic Conflict" (Escalate ε₂), the runtime is not yet fit for purpose as a governance kernel.  9. Decision RecommendationEndorse with these amendments:Implement a versioned namespace strategy (e.g., /ns/core/v0/) immediately.  Expand the AgentCharter class to include explicit Permit/Prohibit properties rather than just "classification" strings.  Formalise the SHACL-SPARQL requirements for ε₁–ε₄ triggers before locking the published shape.  